<article class="env-remark" id="05QJ">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05QJ">Remark <span data-tag="05QJ">7.1.6.18</span> <span class="named">(Limit Functors)</span>.</a> Let $K$ be a simplicial set, let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category which admits $K$-indexed limits, and let $\operatorname{Fun}'( K^{\triangleleft }, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ be the full subcategory of $\operatorname{Fun}( K^{\triangleleft }, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ spanned by the limit diagrams. It follows from Corollary <a data-tag="05QH" href="/tag/05QH">7.1.6.17</a> that the restriction map </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Fun}'( K^{\triangleleft }, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \quad \quad \overline{f} \mapsto \overline{f}|_{K} \]
</div>
<p> admits a section $s: \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}'( K^{\triangleleft }, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. Let $v$ denote the cone point of $K^{\triangleleft }$. Then the composition </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \xrightarrow {s} \operatorname{Fun}'( K^{\triangleleft }, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \xrightarrow { \operatorname{ev}_{v} } \operatorname{\mathcal{C}} \]
</div>
<p> is a functor $T$ which carries each diagram $f: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to a limit $T(f)$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. In fact, we can be more precise: $T$ is a limit functor for $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="05Q9" href="/tag/05Q9">7.1.1.18</a>. More precisely, we observe that precomposition with the tautological map </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  Q: \Delta ^1 \times K \simeq K \star _{K} K \rightarrow \Delta ^0 \star _{ \Delta ^0 } K \simeq K^{\triangleleft } \]
</div>
<p> determines a map </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \epsilon : \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \xrightarrow {s} \operatorname{Fun}'( K^{\triangleleft }, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \subseteq \operatorname{Fun}( K^{\triangleleft }, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \xrightarrow { \circ Q} \operatorname{Fun}(\Delta ^1 \times K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \simeq \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{Fun}(K,\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) ), \]
</div>
<p> carrying each diagram $f$ to a natural transformation $\epsilon _{f}: \underline{ T(f) } \rightarrow f$ which exhibits $T(f)$ as a limit of $f$ (Remark <a data-tag="02WA" href="/tag/02WA">7.1.3.6</a>). It follows from the criterion of Corollary <a data-tag="02FX" href="/tag/02FX">6.2.6.5</a> that $\epsilon $ exhibits $T$ as a right adjoint of the diagonal map $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. </p>
</article>
